#: src/guides/locals.md:1
msgid "Adding Locals Queries"
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:3
msgid ""
"`locals.scm` queries teach Helix about variable scopes and definitions so "
"that local variables can be highlighted distinctly from global ones. When a "
"`@local.reference` identifier is resolved to a `@local.definition`, it "
"inherits the highlight class of that definition rather than the class "
"assigned by `highlights.scm`."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:9
msgid ""
"Query files should be placed in `runtime/queries/{language}/locals.scm` when "
"contributing to Helix. Like other query files, `locals.scm` may reuse "
"another language's with `; inherits: <lang>` on the first line. Locals are "
"resolved in their own pass with their own precedence, independently of "
"`highlights.scm`."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:14
msgid "Captures"
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:16
msgid "`@local.scope`"
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:18
msgid ""
"Marks a node as a scope boundary. Definitions are only visible to references "
"that appear inside the same scope or a nested one. Typical scope nodes are "
"function bodies and blocks."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:29
msgid "`@local.definition.*`"
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:31
msgid ""
"Marks a name node as introducing a local symbol. The suffix after `@local."
"definition.` becomes the highlight class applied to any reference that "
"resolves to this definition. For example, `@local.definition.variable."
"parameter` causes matching references to be highlighted as `variable."
"parameter`."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:43
msgid ""
"Common suffixes mirror the highlight classes in `highlights.scm`: "
"`variable`, `variable.parameter`, `variable.builtin`, `variable.mutable`, "
"`function`, `namespace`, `type`, `constant`, etc."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:47
msgid "`@local.reference`"
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:49
msgid ""
"Marks an identifier node as a potential reference to a local definition. "
"Helix searches enclosing scopes for a matching definition and, if found, "
"highlights the reference with that definition's class instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:57
msgid "Discard captures"
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:59
msgid ""
"Any capture in `locals.scm` that is not `@local.scope`, `@local.reference`, "
"or `@local.definition.*` acts as a discard. It prevents a `@local.reference` "
"from being resolved at that node without affecting the `highlights.scm` "
"result. This is useful for identifier nodes that look like references but "
"should not be treated as variable references, for example keyword argument "
"names or struct field names in struct literals."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:67
msgid "; Keyword argument names in a call are not variable references.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:72
msgid ""
"Later patterns in a query file have higher precedence than earlier ones. A "
"discard pattern must appear after the `@local.reference` pattern it is "
"intended to override. Placing it later ensures it takes precedence and "
"cancels the reference resolution for nodes it matches."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:77
msgid ""
"The convention is to use a capture name beginning with an underscore (e.g. "
"`@_`, `@_keyword`) to make the discard intent clear, but any non-`@local.*` "
"name works."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:80
msgid "How definitions and references are matched"
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:82
msgid ""
"Helix matches references to definitions by comparing the text of the "
"reference node against the text of definitions visible from the current "
"scope. Definitions in inner scopes shadow those in outer scopes. If no "
"definition is found the reference is left with its `highlights.scm` "
"highlight."
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:87
msgid "Relationship with `highlights.scm`"
msgstr ""

#: src/guides/locals.md:89
msgid ""
"The locals system runs alongside `highlights.scm`, not instead of it. "
"`highlights.scm` always determines the baseline highlight for a node. "
"`locals.scm` can override that highlight only when a `@local.reference` "
"successfully resolves to a `@local.definition`, and only for that specific "
"resolution. Non-`@local.*` captures in `locals.scm` (i.e. discards) have no "
"effect on `highlights.scm` results."
msgstr ""
